1
00:00:00,391 --> 00:00:04,024
Section six point three starts with a simple idea.

2
00:00:04,024 --> 00:00:07,592
Here is the hiking map from the count paths video.

3
00:00:07,592 --> 00:00:11,407
In a drawing, the trails can point in any direction.

4
00:00:11,407 --> 00:00:15,443
A topological ordering rearranges the junctions onto a line,

5
00:00:15,443 --> 00:00:19,167
so that every trail points forward, from left to right.

6
00:00:19,167 --> 00:00:21,902
Watch the junctions slide into place.

7
00:00:21,902 --> 00:00:24,063
Every arrow now points right.

8
00:00:24,063 --> 00:00:28,204
Here is a line-up that fails: some arrows point backward.

9
00:00:28,204 --> 00:00:32,553
In symbols: for every edge from vertex u to vertex v,

10
00:00:32,553 --> 00:00:34,102
u comes before v.

11
00:00:34,102 --> 00:00:39,619
The book calls this a total order: any two
vertices have a definite before and after.

12
00:00:40,553 --> 00:00:43,509
Why can a graph with a cycle never have one?

13
00:00:43,509 --> 00:00:48,548
Take three tasks: x before y, y before z, and z before x,

14
00:00:48,548 --> 00:00:49,928
closing the circle.

15
00:00:49,928 --> 00:00:51,243
Who goes first?

16
00:00:51,243 --> 00:00:55,006
Not task x, because task z must come before it.

17
00:00:55,006 --> 00:00:59,082
Not task y, because task x must come before it.

18
00:00:59,082 --> 00:01:03,144
And not task z, because task y must come before it.

19
00:01:03,144 --> 00:01:08,717
Nobody can go first: every vertex on a cycle has an arrow pointing into it.

20
00:01:08,717 --> 00:01:13,808
The book says it from the other end: the
rightmost vertex has an arrow that can only

21
00:01:13,808 --> 00:01:15,045
point left.

22
00:01:15,045 --> 00:01:18,922
So a cycle rules out any topological ordering.

23
00:01:19,851 --> 00:01:23,783
To make this concrete, we will keep using the hiking map:

24
00:01:23,783 --> 00:01:26,817
seven junctions, twelve one-way trails.

25
00:01:26,817 --> 00:01:31,921
This time, every trail has a number: the
scenery points you collect walking it.

26
00:01:31,921 --> 00:01:33,705
We have two goals.

27
00:01:33,705 --> 00:01:37,924
First: an order of the junctions that respects every trail.

28
00:01:37,924 --> 00:01:41,827
Second: the most scenic route from Start to Summit.

29
00:01:42,772 --> 00:01:43,918
First goal.

30
00:01:43,918 --> 00:01:48,645
The book's algorithm uses an array S, with one slot per vertex.

31
00:01:48,645 --> 00:01:51,314
A clock starts at V, which is seven.

32
00:01:51,314 --> 00:01:55,155
Run depth-first search, and whenever a vertex finishes,

33
00:01:55,155 --> 00:01:59,178
put it in the slot the clock points to, then move the clock down.

34
00:01:59,178 --> 00:02:02,759
Summit finishes first: it goes in the last slot,

35
00:02:02,759 --> 00:02:03,814
S seven.

36
00:02:03,814 --> 00:02:06,900
Meadow finishes next, into S six.

37
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:09,048
Falls, into S five.

38
00:02:09,048 --> 00:02:11,118
Creek, into S four.

39
00:02:11,118 --> 00:02:13,150
Ridge, into S three.

40
00:02:13,150 --> 00:02:14,986
Pine, into S two.

41
00:02:14,986 --> 00:02:18,085
And Start finishes last, into S one.

42
00:02:18,085 --> 00:02:22,703
Read the array from left to right, and every trail points to the right.

43
00:02:23,626 --> 00:02:25,944
Lay the vertices out in the order of S.

44
00:02:25,944 --> 00:02:31,777
Start, Pine, Ridge, Creek, Falls, Meadow, Summit.

45
00:02:31,777 --> 00:02:35,905
All twelve trails point to the right: zero point left.

46
00:02:35,905 --> 00:02:40,224
One depth-first search did all of it, so the time is V plus E.

47
00:02:41,108 --> 00:02:44,779
In Python, the simplest version needs no clock.

48
00:02:44,779 --> 00:02:48,764
Visit a vertex by visiting every neighbor not yet visited.

49
00:02:48,764 --> 00:02:53,048
Then append the vertex to a list, after all its neighbors.

50
00:02:53,048 --> 00:02:55,639
That puts the vertices in finish order.

51
00:02:55,639 --> 00:03:00,744
The last line reverses the list in place,
which is the same as filling the array from

52
00:03:00,744 --> 00:03:01,668
the back.

53
00:03:01,668 --> 00:03:06,352
And an outer loop starts a search from every vertex not yet visited.

54
00:03:07,254 --> 00:03:10,249
This version assumes the graph has no cycle.

55
00:03:10,249 --> 00:03:14,259
If we already know it is acyclic, plain marking is enough.

56
00:03:14,259 --> 00:03:18,660
If it might have a cycle, use the three-state version from the last video,

57
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:23,218
and fail gracefully the moment an edge leads to an active vertex.

58
00:03:23,218 --> 00:03:27,081
The book writes that failure as: fail gracefully.

59
00:03:27,989 --> 00:03:31,062
Often, the sorted list is not the real goal.

60
00:03:31,062 --> 00:03:34,213
We want to do something at each vertex, in order.

61
00:03:34,213 --> 00:03:37,911
Then we can do that something at the end of each vertex's search,

62
00:03:37,911 --> 00:03:41,674
which handles the vertices in reverse topological order.

63
00:03:41,674 --> 00:03:47,286
No array is needed: the book just renames the post-visit step to process.

64
00:03:47,286 --> 00:03:50,645
Summit is processed first, then Meadow, Falls, Creek,

65
00:03:50,645 --> 00:03:53,237
Ridge, Pine, and Start last.

66
00:03:53,237 --> 00:03:56,853
Every vertex is processed after everything it points to.

67
00:03:57,757 --> 00:04:01,156
What if we want the vertices in forward order instead?

68
00:04:01,156 --> 00:04:05,765
One way: search the reversal of the graph, written rev of G,

69
00:04:05,765 --> 00:04:07,705
where every edge is flipped.

70
00:04:07,705 --> 00:04:12,926
Every source of G becomes a sink of rev of G, and the other way round.

71
00:04:12,926 --> 00:04:18,656
A reversed cycle is still a cycle, so reversing a DAG gives another DAG.

72
00:04:18,656 --> 00:04:22,428
And building the reversal takes V plus E time.

73
00:04:23,390 --> 00:04:26,750
There is a subtle point, in the book's footnote.

74
00:04:26,750 --> 00:04:29,757
Here is the reverse of G's postorder.

75
00:04:29,757 --> 00:04:33,156
And here is a postorder of the reversed graph.

76
00:04:33,156 --> 00:04:38,091
Both are topological orderings of G, with zero backward edges.

77
00:04:38,091 --> 00:04:42,088
But they are different: Ridge and Creek swap places.

78
00:04:42,088 --> 00:04:48,204
So a postordering of rev of G is not
necessarily the reversal of a postordering of G.

79
00:04:49,139 --> 00:04:51,183
Section six point four.

80
00:04:51,183 --> 00:04:52,577
Remember the frog.

81
00:04:52,577 --> 00:04:56,913
The ways from stone i is the ways from stone i plus one,

82
00:04:56,913 --> 00:04:59,517
plus the ways from stone i plus two.

83
00:04:59,517 --> 00:05:02,941
The book draws a dependency graph for any recurrence:

84
00:05:02,941 --> 00:05:07,707
a vertex for every subproblem, and an
edge from one subproblem to another,

85
00:05:07,707 --> 00:05:10,650
if evaluating the first needs the second.

86
00:05:10,650 --> 00:05:14,881
For the frog, that graph is the stepping-stone graph itself.

87
00:05:14,881 --> 00:05:16,926
And it has to be acyclic.

88
00:05:16,926 --> 00:05:20,008
Otherwise, the plain recursion would never halt.

89
00:05:20,964 --> 00:05:22,735
Here is the big claim.

90
00:05:22,735 --> 00:05:27,852
Evaluating a recurrence with memoization
is exactly the same as a depth-first search

91
00:05:27,852 --> 00:05:29,597
of the dependency graph.

92
00:05:29,597 --> 00:05:31,706
Is the value still undefined?

93
00:05:31,706 --> 00:05:35,574
That is the same as: is the vertex unmarked.

94
00:05:35,574 --> 00:05:38,113
Starting to evaluate is the pre-visit.

95
00:05:38,113 --> 00:05:43,035
Evaluating every subproblem it needs is the recursive calls along the edges.

96
00:05:43,035 --> 00:05:45,873
And finishing the value is the post-visit.

97
00:05:45,873 --> 00:05:49,584
A vertex is marked when its value has been computed.

98
00:05:49,584 --> 00:05:54,203
The pre-visit and post-visit steps stand in for the actual arithmetic.

99
00:05:55,135 --> 00:05:57,062
Now dynamic programming.

100
00:05:57,062 --> 00:06:01,385
It evaluates every subproblem in reverse topological order:

101
00:06:01,385 --> 00:06:04,927
each one is considered after the subproblems it depends on.

102
00:06:04,927 --> 00:06:08,104
For the frog, that means stone seven, six, five,

103
00:06:08,104 --> 00:06:09,341
down to zero.

104
00:06:09,341 --> 00:06:12,830
That order is a postorder of the dependency graph.

105
00:06:12,830 --> 00:06:18,156
So every dynamic programming algorithm
is equivalent to a postorder traversal of the

106
00:06:18,156 --> 00:06:20,600
dependency graph of its recurrence.

107
00:06:21,545 --> 00:06:26,155
Most dynamic programming algorithms
look different from a topological sort.

108
00:06:26,155 --> 00:06:27,821
Take edit distance.

109
00:06:27,821 --> 00:06:32,665
Each subproblem depends on its left, upper, and diagonal neighbors.

110
00:06:32,665 --> 00:06:35,243
The dependency graph is a regular grid.

111
00:06:35,243 --> 00:06:39,514
Because it is so regular, we hard-wire a good order into the algorithm,

112
00:06:39,514 --> 00:06:44,110
as nested loops, so no topological sort is needed at run time.

113
00:06:44,110 --> 00:06:47,075
The book calls that order the evaluation order.

114
00:06:48,016 --> 00:06:53,316
So there are two small differences between most
dynamic programming and topological sort.

115
00:06:53,316 --> 00:06:56,193
First, the dependency graph is implicit.

116
00:06:56,193 --> 00:07:01,297
The recurrence encodes the edges, so there is no adjacency list in memory.

117
00:07:01,297 --> 00:07:06,415
That is minor, as long as we can list each
subproblem's children in constant time.

118
00:07:06,415 --> 00:07:12,951
Second, the graph is usually regular, so
nested loops replace the topological sort.

119
00:07:12,951 --> 00:07:17,596
When the graph is not regular, we can use depth-first search directly.

120
00:07:18,569 --> 00:07:22,150
Here is an irregular graph: the hiking map.

121
00:07:22,150 --> 00:07:26,486
The longest path problem asks: given a weighted directed graph,

122
00:07:26,486 --> 00:07:31,707
a start s and a target t, find the path with the largest total weight.

123
00:07:31,707 --> 00:07:33,739
Here, the most scenic route.

124
00:07:33,739 --> 00:07:37,801
In general directed graphs, that problem is NP-hard.

125
00:07:37,801 --> 00:07:41,847
But if the graph is acyclic, it takes only linear time.

126
00:07:42,795 --> 00:07:49,475
For any vertex v, let LLP of v be the length of the longest path from v to t.

127
00:07:49,475 --> 00:07:53,134
If v is t, it is zero: you are already there.

128
00:07:53,134 --> 00:07:57,105
Otherwise, look at every edge from v to some w.

129
00:07:57,105 --> 00:08:02,313
Take the edge's length, plus LLP of w, and keep the maximum.

130
00:08:02,313 --> 00:08:09,019
If v has no edges out, and is not t, the
maximum of nothing is minus infinity:

131
00:08:09,019 --> 00:08:10,425
there is no route.

132
00:08:10,425 --> 00:08:14,709
The dependency graph of this recurrence is the input graph itself.

133
00:08:14,709 --> 00:08:20,812
So a depth-first search, memoizing as we go, solves it in V plus E time.

134
00:08:21,665 --> 00:08:22,591
Run it.

135
00:08:22,591 --> 00:08:25,898
Summit is the target, so its score is zero.

136
00:08:25,898 --> 00:08:29,986
Meadow has one trail, to Summit, worth five.

137
00:08:29,986 --> 00:08:32,174
Five plus zero is five.

138
00:08:32,174 --> 00:08:35,012
Falls has one trail, worth four.

139
00:08:35,012 --> 00:08:35,950
Four.

140
00:08:35,950 --> 00:08:37,864
Creek has two trails.

141
00:08:37,864 --> 00:08:41,002
Via Meadow: two plus five is seven.

142
00:08:41,002 --> 00:08:44,192
Via Falls: seven plus four is eleven.

143
00:08:44,192 --> 00:08:45,664
The best is eleven.

144
00:08:45,664 --> 00:08:49,218
Ridge: via Meadow, four plus five is nine.

145
00:08:49,218 --> 00:08:52,148
Via Falls, three plus four is seven.

146
00:08:52,148 --> 00:08:53,671
The best is nine.

147
00:08:53,671 --> 00:08:55,846
Pine has three trails.

148
00:08:55,846 --> 00:08:59,010
Via Creek: five plus eleven is sixteen.

149
00:08:59,010 --> 00:09:02,395
Via Ridge: one plus nine is ten.

150
00:09:02,395 --> 00:09:05,520
Via Meadow: six plus five is eleven.

151
00:09:05,520 --> 00:09:07,148
The best is sixteen.

152
00:09:07,148 --> 00:09:11,536
Start: via Pine, three plus sixteen is nineteen.

153
00:09:11,536 --> 00:09:14,700
Via Creek, four plus eleven is fifteen.

154
00:09:14,700 --> 00:09:17,877
Via Ridge, two plus nine is eleven.

155
00:09:17,877 --> 00:09:19,618
The best is nineteen.

156
00:09:20,574 --> 00:09:22,072
Now the Python.

157
00:09:22,072 --> 00:09:25,913
The function takes the graph, a dictionary called length,

158
00:09:25,913 --> 00:09:31,941
whose keys are pairs: the pair v, w gives the points of the trail from v to w.

159
00:09:31,941 --> 00:09:34,142
And the start and the target.

160
00:09:34,142 --> 00:09:37,172
Best is an empty dictionary: the cache.

161
00:09:38,069 --> 00:09:40,543
Inside, the helper function go.

162
00:09:40,543 --> 00:09:43,447
If v is t, return zero.

163
00:09:43,447 --> 00:09:47,171
If v is not in best yet, we have not computed it.

164
00:09:47,171 --> 00:09:48,785
Only then do the work.

165
00:09:48,785 --> 00:09:52,948
Otherwise, skip down to the last line, and return what is stored.

166
00:09:53,882 --> 00:09:55,354
Now the heart.

167
00:09:55,354 --> 00:10:01,669
Start best of v at minus infinity: float of
the text minus inf makes a special number,

168
00:10:01,669 --> 00:10:06,070
smaller than any other, which is the book's maximum of nothing.

169
00:10:06,070 --> 00:10:11,265
Then for each trail out of v, to a vertex w: look up the trail's points,

170
00:10:11,265 --> 00:10:15,978
using the pair v, w, add go of w, the best score from there,

171
00:10:15,978 --> 00:10:19,139
and keep the larger of that and the best so far.

172
00:10:20,052 --> 00:10:21,628
Return best of v.

173
00:10:21,628 --> 00:10:25,964
And the last line starts everything: return go of s.

174
00:10:25,964 --> 00:10:28,737
That is the book's LongestPath algorithm.

175
00:10:28,737 --> 00:10:33,555
Each vertex is computed once, and each trail is looked at once:

176
00:10:33,555 --> 00:10:34,801
V plus E.

177
00:10:35,723 --> 00:10:38,796
The answer is nineteen scenery points.

178
00:10:38,796 --> 00:10:45,424
Start to Pine, to Creek, to Falls, to Summit:
three plus five plus seven plus four.

179
00:10:45,424 --> 00:10:48,093
One extra, which is not in the book.

180
00:10:48,093 --> 00:10:50,619
The algorithm computes the length.

181
00:10:50,619 --> 00:10:56,279
To recover the route itself, remember which
trail gave the maximum at each junction.

182
00:10:57,176 --> 00:11:01,187
The book then says: we can turn this into dynamic programming,

183
00:11:01,187 --> 00:11:03,088
with a topological sort.

184
00:11:03,088 --> 00:11:05,470
Take the finish order from the depth-first search,

185
00:11:05,470 --> 00:11:06,759
and loop through it.

186
00:11:06,759 --> 00:11:10,627
Children always come before parents, so when we reach a vertex,

187
00:11:10,627 --> 00:11:13,687
every best of w it needs is already in the table,

188
00:11:13,687 --> 00:11:15,679
and no recursion is needed.

189
00:11:15,679 --> 00:11:19,298
Watch the table fill in: Summit, zero.

190
00:11:19,298 --> 00:11:20,965
Meadow, five.

191
00:11:20,965 --> 00:11:22,306
Falls, four.

192
00:11:22,306 --> 00:11:23,778
Creek, eleven.

193
00:11:23,778 --> 00:11:25,301
Ridge, nine.

194
00:11:25,301 --> 00:11:26,786
Pine, sixteen.

195
00:11:26,786 --> 00:11:28,322
Start, nineteen.

196
00:11:28,322 --> 00:11:32,905
The same table, in the same order, as the memoized version.

197
00:11:32,905 --> 00:11:37,837
Memoization and dynamic programming are two views of one postorder.

198
00:11:38,756 --> 00:11:42,806
To recap sections six point three and six point four.

199
00:11:42,806 --> 00:11:47,597
A topological order is the reverse of a depth-first search's finishing order.

200
00:11:47,597 --> 00:11:52,741
To process in reverse order, process each vertex as it finishes.

201
00:11:52,741 --> 00:11:56,191
For forward order, search the reversed graph.

202
00:11:56,191 --> 00:12:00,045
Memoization is a depth-first search of the dependency graph,

203
00:12:00,045 --> 00:12:02,024
which must be acyclic.

204
00:12:02,024 --> 00:12:05,709
And dynamic programming is a postorder of that same graph,

205
00:12:05,709 --> 00:12:09,915
so longest path in a DAG takes V plus E time.

206
00:12:09,915 --> 00:12:13,769
Pause, and say each of those four in your own words.

207
00:12:13,769 --> 00:12:16,135
That is a good start for your learning log.
